[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Text vs Keyword Fields and Normalization in Elasticsearch"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When designing an Elasticsearch index, choosing the correct data type for string fields is critical for search performance, relevance, and aggregation accuracy. Elasticsearch provides two primary core types for strings: text and keyword."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Text Field Type"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The text type is designed for full-text search. When you index a text field, Elasticsearch runs the input through an analyzer. This process tokenizes the sentence into individual words (terms), converts them to lowercase, and removes stop words. This allows users to search for individual words within a large body of text."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"PUT /my_index
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "description": {
        "type": "text"
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"The Keyword Field type"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The keyword type is designed for structured content such as email addresses, hostnames, status codes, tags, and zip codes. Keyword fields are not analyzed. Instead, the exact string is indexed as a single term. This makes them ideal for exact-value matching, sorting, and aggregations."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"PUT /my_index
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "status": {
        "type": "keyword"
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Normalization in Keyword Fields"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"While keyword fields bypass standard text analyzers, you often want to apply minimal transformations like lowercase conversion or trimming whitespace before indexing. This process is called normalization. Normalizers operate similarly to analyzers, but they must emit a single token and cannot use tokenizers."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"PUT /normalized_index
{
  "settings": {
    "analysis": {
      "normalizer": {
        "my_normalizer": {
          "type": "custom",
          "char_filter": [],
          "filter": ["lowercase", "asciifolding"]
        }
      }
    }
  },
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "product_code": {
        "type": "keyword",
        "normalizer": "my_normalizer"
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Multi-Fields: Best of Both Worlds"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"In many scenarios, you need to perform full-text search and aggregations on the same field. Elasticsearch solves this via multi-fields, allowing you to index a single string value as both text and keyword."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"PUT /my_index
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "title": {
        "type": "text",
        "fields": {
          "keyword": {
            "type": "keyword",
            "ignore_above": 256
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use text for fields requiring linguistic analysis, stemming, and full-text search."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use keyword for exact matching, filtering, sorting, and aggregations."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Apply normalizers to keyword fields to standardize data consistency (e.g., lowercase)."}]
